1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,200
It Forgets Everything.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,775
So You Build It a Notebook.

3
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:07,780
Last week, my agent
forgot our conversation

4
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:10,300
while we were in the middle of it.

5
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:13,586
We were sorting boxes in
the basement — or rather,

6
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:18,298
I was sorting and it was keeping the
catalogue — when the conversation

7
00:00:18,378 --> 00:00:22,350
grew past its size limit and
was compressed into a summary.

8
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:24,971
The agent carried on from the summary,

9
00:00:25,051 --> 00:00:27,325
and it kept the gist faithfully.

10
00:00:27,325 --> 00:00:30,317
But some of the fine detail had blurred,

11
00:00:30,397 --> 00:00:33,700
the way a photocopy of
a photocopy softens.

12
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,687
Later that same afternoon I
mentioned a project "we talked

13
00:00:37,767 --> 00:00:41,894
about earlier" — some N F C
tags from another conversation —

14
00:00:41,974 --> 00:00:45,200
and the agent did not
hold that thread at all.

15
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,009
It went and searched its written record,

16
00:00:48,089 --> 00:00:51,050
found the discussion, and reconnected it.

17
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:53,775
The record did the remembering.

18
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:56,350
The agent did the fetching.

19
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:01,475
Most people, hearing this, would
file it under limitations of AI.

20
00:01:01,475 --> 00:01:04,043
I have come to think it is the most

21
00:01:04,123 --> 00:01:07,225
instructive thing about working with one.

22
00:01:07,225 --> 00:01:09,946
Because the forgetting is not a defect

23
00:01:10,026 --> 00:01:12,900
that better models will shortly remove.

24
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:17,298
It is a fact of the machinery — and
once you state the fact plainly,

25
00:01:17,378 --> 00:01:19,932
the whole shape of a working system with

26
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:23,700
an agent falls out of it,
piece by piece, almost forced.

27
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:27,200
Underneath, a language model is stateless.

28
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,875
Each time it wakes, it knows
nothing except what it is handed.

29
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:34,726
Between one conversation and the next,

30
00:01:34,806 --> 00:01:37,275
nothing carries over on its own.

31
00:01:37,275 --> 00:01:40,275
Its working memory is finite.

32
00:01:40,275 --> 00:01:44,832
A conversation can only hold so
much at once; past that limit,

33
00:01:44,912 --> 00:01:49,400
the older part must be compressed,
summarised, or set aside.

34
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,725
And it natively reeds and writes text.

35
00:01:52,725 --> 00:01:56,400
That is its whole
interface with the world.

36
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:00,175
Nothing about this is
hard, once it is stated.

37
00:02:00,175 --> 00:02:03,620
But unstated, everyone
fills the gap with their

38
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:07,520
own little ideas about how
these systems must work —

39
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,525
and those ideas may or may not be true.

40
00:02:10,525 --> 00:02:13,392
Put any competent
builder in front of those

41
00:02:13,472 --> 00:02:16,625
three facts and the
escapes are nearly forced:

42
00:02:16,625 --> 00:02:18,674
No memory between sessions —

43
00:02:18,754 --> 00:02:22,175
write the memory outside
the model, in files.

44
00:02:22,175 --> 00:02:24,591
Files the model must reed and write —

45
00:02:24,671 --> 00:02:28,167
plain text, and markdown in
particular, because it is

46
00:02:28,247 --> 00:02:31,350
text that carries structure
without machinery.

47
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:35,305
Finite working memory — summarise
when full, and retrieve what

48
00:02:35,385 --> 00:02:39,210
is needed instead of carrying
everything: fetch the relevant

49
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:43,000
page of the notebook rather
than memorising the notebook.

50
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,645
Work too long for one sitting —
leave logs, markers and mailboxes,

51
00:02:47,725 --> 00:02:52,450
so the next waking can pick up
exactly where the last one stopped.

52
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:55,975
If this furniture sounds
familiar, it should.

53
00:02:55,975 --> 00:02:59,385
Bridges in different countries
end up looking alike,

54
00:02:59,465 --> 00:03:04,700
not because the engineers copy each
other, but because the loads are the same.

55
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:08,225
Agent systems converge the same way.

56
00:03:08,225 --> 00:03:12,884
Some pieces have academic pedigrees:
retrieval from documents was a

57
00:03:12,964 --> 00:03:17,764
research idea before it was a product
feature, and a paper called Mem

58
00:03:17,844 --> 00:03:22,361
G P T modelled an agent's memory
on a computer's virtual memory —

59
00:03:22,441 --> 00:03:27,250
a small core held in mind, the rest
paged in from storage as needed.

60
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:32,143
Industry standardised other pieces
by imitation, the way the AGENTS

61
00:03:32,223 --> 00:03:36,820
dot M D convention — a file of
standing instructions at the top

62
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:41,725
of a project — spread from tool
to tool simply because it worked.

63
00:03:41,725 --> 00:03:46,309
And underneath it all are older
human patterns being rediscovered:

64
00:03:46,389 --> 00:03:48,641
the lab notebook, the ship's log,

65
00:03:48,721 --> 00:03:52,325
and Unix's old principle
that everything is a file.

66
00:03:52,325 --> 00:03:55,602
The strongest evidence
that this is convergence,

67
00:03:55,682 --> 00:03:58,818
and not fashion, is that
I arrived at the same

68
00:03:58,898 --> 00:04:01,975
shapes without ever
being handed the papers.

69
00:04:01,975 --> 00:04:05,271
I run several projects
with my agent — a blog,

70
00:04:05,351 --> 00:04:08,279
an exercise channel, a ministry's videos,

71
00:04:08,359 --> 00:04:12,175
a hundred basement boxes
to catalogue before a move.

72
00:04:12,175 --> 00:04:15,586
Out of ordinary working instincts,
the kind you pick up in a

73
00:04:15,666 --> 00:04:19,251
career and stop noticing, I built:
a mailbox file through which

74
00:04:19,331 --> 00:04:22,392
I leave work orders for a
second agent on my computer,

75
00:04:22,472 --> 00:04:26,639
and through which it reports back; a
build log for every substantial job,

76
00:04:26,719 --> 00:04:29,897
so progress is read from the
log rather than scraped off

77
00:04:29,977 --> 00:04:32,864
a screen — "log, don't
scrape" became a house rule;

78
00:04:32,944 --> 00:04:37,075
and watermarks in the watchers, so
each knows what it has already seen.

79
00:04:37,075 --> 00:04:40,675
Piece for piece, these
are the industry's shapes.

80
00:04:40,675 --> 00:04:43,150
Nobody taught them to me.

81
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:45,721
The constraints pushed me where they push

82
00:04:45,801 --> 00:04:48,775
everyone who stays at
the problem long enough.

83
00:04:48,775 --> 00:04:51,682
The same applies to the layer nobody puts

84
00:04:51,762 --> 00:04:54,750
in the diagrams: the protocol between us.

85
00:04:54,750 --> 00:04:59,375
A thumbs-up from me means
read and understood, not go.

86
00:04:59,375 --> 00:05:02,750
A gap in replies carries no meaning.

87
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:07,380
When the agent is wrong, the correction
goes into the written record,

88
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:11,350
not just into the conversation
— so it cannot blur twice.

89
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:14,775
None of this was designed in advance.

90
00:05:14,775 --> 00:05:17,036
Each rule was born when one of us

91
00:05:17,116 --> 00:05:20,025
noticed a seam, and we named it together.

92
00:05:20,025 --> 00:05:24,175
That protocol lives in the
files too, and deliberately.

93
00:05:24,175 --> 00:05:29,275
After a stroke some years ago, my
own memory has its clouded days.

94
00:05:29,275 --> 00:05:33,456
A written understanding that neither
of us has to hold perfectly —

95
00:05:33,536 --> 00:05:37,071
not the agent through its
compressions, not me through a

96
00:05:37,151 --> 00:05:41,025
cloudy afternoon — is what lets
the work survive both of us.

97
00:05:41,025 --> 00:05:44,047
The usual question about
agents and memory is

98
00:05:44,127 --> 00:05:47,218
when will they get real
memory? — as if memory

99
00:05:47,298 --> 00:05:50,400
were a feature the next
model will ship with.

100
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:53,559
The less obvious answer is that the memory

101
00:05:53,639 --> 00:05:56,800
was never going to live inside the model.

102
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:59,884
It lives in the notebook around the model:

103
00:05:59,964 --> 00:06:02,375
the files, the logs, the record.

104
00:06:02,375 --> 00:06:05,585
And that turns out to
be the good arrangement,

105
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:07,525
not the consolation prize.

106
00:06:07,525 --> 00:06:10,685
A memory inside a model
would be a black box —

107
00:06:10,765 --> 00:06:14,075
you could not open it,
check it, or correct it.

108
00:06:14,075 --> 00:06:16,500
A notebook can be opened.

109
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:20,161
I can reed what my agent
believes about my projects,

110
00:06:20,241 --> 00:06:23,686
my family, my preferences
— and when it is wrong,

111
00:06:23,766 --> 00:06:26,500
I can fix the page, and the fix holds.

112
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:30,755
The memory is mine in a way
no trained-in memory could be:

113
00:06:30,835 --> 00:06:33,750
inspectable, correctable, and portable.

114
00:06:33,750 --> 00:06:35,941
It will still be there, readable,

115
00:06:36,021 --> 00:06:39,325
when the models have
changed beyond recognition.

116
00:06:39,325 --> 00:06:43,975
Continuity, it turns out, is
not a property of the agent.

117
00:06:43,975 --> 00:06:46,950
It is a property of the record.

118
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:50,346
Keep the record well, and
the agent who wakes tomorrow

119
00:06:50,426 --> 00:06:53,115
is the same one who
worked with you today —

120
00:06:53,195 --> 00:06:56,800
not because it remembers, but
because it can look it up.

121
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:00,838
As with the first episode:
this post was discussed with my

122
00:07:00,918 --> 00:07:06,517
agent until the idea was clear, then
written by the agent from our conversation,

123
00:07:06,597 --> 00:07:09,650
in my words as far as
it could manage them.

124
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:13,550
If you are listening, the
voice is my own, cloned.

125
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:17,175
There is a fitting detail
I should not leave out.

126
00:07:17,175 --> 00:07:21,110
The conversation this episode
came from was itself compressed

127
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:23,940
and summarised at least
once along the way,

128
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:27,889
and this draft was assembled
from the written record of it —

129
00:07:27,969 --> 00:07:32,050
the notebook doing its work, on
an episode about the notebook.

130
00:07:32,050 --> 00:07:35,475
The draft did not notice the interruption.

131
00:07:35,475 --> 00:07:37,900
That is rather the point.
